  <h2>The Rule: Waist Less Than Half Your Height</h2>
  <p>If your waist circumference is less than half your height, you are in a healthy cardiometabolic range. The same cutoff (0.5) applies across men, women, and ethnic groups — a striking simplicity in a field full of population-specific thresholds. Above 0.5, cardiometabolic risk begins to rise. Above 0.6, risk is substantially elevated.</p>
  <p>Margaret Ashwell and Shigeo Hsieh proposed this single-cutoff approach in 2005 in the <em>International Journal of Food Sciences and Nutrition</em>, arguing that the simplicity had public-health value beyond what BMI's sex- and ethnicity-specific categories could offer.</p>

  <h2>Risk Thresholds</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>WHtR</th><th>Risk level</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>&lt; 0.50</td><td>Healthy</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.50–0.59</td><td>Increased risk</td></tr>
      <tr><td>≥ 0.60</td><td>Substantially elevated risk</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Why a Single Cutoff Works Across Populations</h2>
  <p>BMI cutoffs are population-specific because the BMI–fat mass relationship differs by sex and ethnicity. WHtR's single cutoff works because it normalizes waist by height, removing the height-dependent bias that affects BMI. Once height is accounted for, the cardiometabolic risk associated with a given proportion of visceral fat appears to be remarkably consistent across populations.</p>
  <p>Comparative studies have found WHtR to be at least as good as BMI at identifying cardiometabolic risk — and in some studies better, particularly in populations where BMI cutoffs are unreliable (Asian, pediatric, older adult).</p>

  <h2>What About WHR?</h2>
  <p>Waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) is sex-specific — different cutoffs for men and women. WHtR uses the same single cutoff across sexes. This makes WHtR simpler to communicate: "your waist should be less than half your height" requires no sex-specific lookup table.</p>
  <p>For individual cardiometabolic risk assessment, both WHR and WHtR add information beyond BMI. For population screening and public-health messaging, WHtR is the more accessible metric.</p>

  <h2>Children and Adolescents</h2>
  <p>WHtR is particularly useful in pediatric populations because it does not require age- and sex-specific reference curves the way BMI-for-age percentiles do. The single 0.5 cutoff applies from age 5 through adulthood — a major practical advantage for screening in school or clinical settings.</p>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <p>WHtR is a screening tool, not a diagnostic. It does not distinguish between subcutaneous and visceral fat with full precision, and it is insensitive to muscle mass at the waist (relevant in highly muscular athletes). Use it alongside other clinical markers for individual decisions.</p>

